   "parts": [
    [
     1,
     "\r\n\r\n \r\n  \r\n  \r\n \r\n <div style=\"word-break:break-word\"><div style=\"line-height:1;padding:0.5em\">Hi,<br><br>there are three other dashed arrows: the curved arrows with two arrowheads pointing from NS, NS and SS to the variables themselves. These would not be seen e.g. in Amos (and of course no error variables, which are used there as representation). Wouldn&#39;t it be more correct to say that the dashed arrows represent covariances between exogenous variables and their variances?<br><br></div><div style=\"line-height:1;padding:0.3em\"><div>Am 30.01.21, 09:30 schrieb &quot;G. S. Ashiabi&quot; &lt;<a href data-email-masked rel=\"nofollow\">ashi...@gmail.com</a>&gt;:</div><blockquote class=\"gmail_quote\" style=\"margin:0.8ex 0pt 0pt 0.8ex;border-left:1px solid rgb(204,204,204);padding-left:1ex\"></blockquote></div></div>"
